# Developing this literate program

The book is the source of the program. Edit its explanations and code fragments
together, then regenerate the TypeScript and HTML. VS Code is useful for browsing
fragments, but it is not required to build or verify this repository.

## Bootstrap from a checkout

Use Node.js 22 LTS and the Yarn version pinned in `package.json`. Enable Corepack
if necessary, then install the locked dependencies:

```sh
yarn install --immutable
yarn build:cli
```

The checked-in generated TypeScript is the bootstrap: `build:cli` bundles only
the CLI's local modules into `out/cli.js`, using installed packages at runtime.
It does not type-check or compile
the extension, so an extension error does not prevent generation. No global
TypeScript compiler or published Literate extension is required. `.gitattributes`
keeps text checkouts in LF form, matching the engine's deterministic output even
on Windows. Output names follow a portable path policy: case-only collisions,
Windows device names, and ambiguous trailing dots or spaces are rejected.

## The editing loop

The shared engine is explained in [Headless generation engine](engine.html), the
filesystem adapter in [Command-line generation](cli.html), and the editor adapter
in [Literate Programming](literate.html). Change those `.literate` chapters, not
`src/engine.ts`, `src/cli.ts`, `src/self-hosting.ts`, or `src/extension.ts`
directly. The grabber is also generated. Tests, package configuration, templates, and styles remain ordinary
source files.

```sh
yarn literate generate
yarn test
yarn esbuild
```

`yarn literate` bundles the currently checked-in/generated CLI before running it. `generate` writes source and HTML only when their bytes change.
`yarn test` compiles the new implementation, lints it, runs headless regression
tests, and checks that generation agrees with all the files on disk.
`yarn esbuild` bundles the extension's actual entry point, `out/main.js`.

Review the generated diff as well as the chapter changes. Prose-only edits should
change HTML but not TypeScript. A reorganization intended to preserve the program
should not alter its generated source.

## Check without writing

```sh
yarn literate check
```

The check command regenerates in memory and reports missing or stale files. It
never updates the book or generated outputs. This is also available as
`yarn check:generated`. The optional final argument selects another project root:

```sh
yarn literate generate /path/to/a/test-project
yarn literate check /path/to/a/test-project
```

Both source and HTML outputs are checked. Orphaned output files are not detected
or deleted: removing a root fragment or renaming an output requires an explicit
review of the old file.

## Changing the generator itself

A generator change needs two generations. First the previous executable produces
the new source. Rebuild it and verify that the new executable reproduces that
source and its documentation:

```sh
yarn literate generate
yarn literate self-check
yarn test
```

`self-check` requires current outputs, type-checks freshly generated TypeScript
through an in-memory overlay, then builds and runs a fresh engine in a temporary
child process. Its output paths and bytes must match the current generation plan.
It does not write project files or silently repair stale outputs. This is an
explicit engine fixed-point check, not a replacement for CLI runtime tests.
Use it only on trusted code; the child is not a security sandbox.

If the new generator deliberately changes formatting, the check will report the
old output. Run `yarn literate generate` again, review the differences, then repeat
the check. Keep generated TypeScript committed so the next checkout can bootstrap.

If newly generated CLI code fails to build, keep using a known-working executable
with `node out/cli.js generate` to regenerate after fixing the literate source.
Do not repair generated TypeScript by hand. Keep a separate known-good CLI bundle
before risky generator syntax changes; `out/cli.js` can also be overwritten by
compilation, so it is not a permanent backup.

For syntax changes, retain compatibility long enough to generate the new parser
before migrating the book. During this initial extraction the generated engine
was bootstrapped once; subsequent changes use the same literate-first workflow.

## Inspection for authors and tools

```sh
yarn literate inspect --json
yarn literate check --json
yarn literate self-check --json
```

Inspection lists ordered input documents, referenced templates, and each fragment's
output, definitions, and dependencies. Locations refer to the literate sources,
not generated files. JSON responses use schema version 1 and zero-based positions;
usage errors and I/O failures are also structured. Inspection and checks never
write project output. The `yarn literate` wrapper does rebuild `out/cli.js`; invoke
`node out/cli.js` directly when the whole invocation must avoid project writes.

## Safe generation while editing

A missing fragment, a cycle, an invalid declaration, or an unsafe output path is
an error. The engine returns diagnostics and no output files, preserving the last
successful generated program. Fragments are validated even when unused. Output
paths must remain within the project, and outputs cannot overwrite inputs or
collide with one another.

A literal opening delimiter inside a code fragment is written as `\<<`. It becomes
`<<` in generated source and documentation, without triggering expansion. This
allows the generator to describe and implement its own fragment syntax without
special-case exceptions. Unescaped fragment references are expanded even inside
strings or comments; this is a text generator, not a programming-language parser.

The CLI reads saved files. The VS Code adapter uses open editor buffers, including
empty buffers, and automatically generates on literate content changes. It shares
the engine with the CLI; completion refreshes do not write files. Template-only
edits currently require `Literate: Process`. Do not run CLI generation concurrently
with editor generation against the same project or leave unsaved literate buffers
open while checking disk-based results.

Validation precedes output writes, but writing several files is not a filesystem
transaction. An I/O failure can leave earlier writes in place. Resolve the error
and generate again. The CLI rejects symlink targets and skips symlink inputs;
the VS Code adapter uses the workspace filesystem provider instead.

## Testing the editor

Headless tests exercise the engine, CLI, and a mocked VS Code adapter without a
GUI or downloads. They do not replace testing the real extension host.

Disable the published extension in this repository: older generators do not
understand the explicit literal-delimiter escapes used by the new chapters.
Bootstrap with the CLI, then debug the newly built extension.

For interactive debugging, run `yarn esbuild-watch` in a terminal, select **Run
Extension**, and press **F5**. The existing launch task compiles TypeScript but
does not bundle `out/main.js`, so the watcher is important. Use a disposable test
workspace and associate `*.literate` with Markdown for editor providers.

`yarn test:extension` builds the extension and invokes the existing VS Code test
runner. That runner may download VS Code and requires an appropriate GUI/display
environment. Its original sample test is not a substitute for the headless
regression suite or an interactive smoke test.
